WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.372
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:10.349
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.649 --> 00:00:17.585
Unit twenty-four: Manufacturing and Garments. Today you will talk about services done for us with the causative have / get.

4
00:00:17.885 --> 00:00:20.584
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:24.778
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:26.827
Manufacturing.

7
00:00:27.817 --> 00:00:32.289
Manufacturing means the making of goods in large numbers, usually in factories.

8
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:35.487
Manufacturing employs many young workers.

9
00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:39.107
Your turn. Say: Manufacturing.

10
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:43.432
Manufacturing.

11
00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:44.917
Garment.

12
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:47.765
Garment means a piece of clothing.

13
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.686
Each garment is checked before it is packed.

14
00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:53.553
Your turn. Say: Garment.

15
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.076
Garment.

16
00:00:57.817 --> 00:00:58.748
Factory.

17
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.607
Factory means a building where goods are made by machines and workers.

18
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:06.181
The factory opened a new training room.

19
00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:09.308
Your turn. Say: Factory.

20
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:13.052
Factory.

21
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:14.771
Export.

22
00:01:15.783 --> 00:01:18.584
Export means a product sold to another country.

23
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:21.496
Garments are the country's top export.

24
00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:24.469
Your turn. Say: Export.

25
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:28.137
Export.

26
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:29.884
Workers.

27
00:01:30.883 --> 00:01:34.299
Workers means people who do a job, especially manual work.

28
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:37.173
Workers asked for safer conditions.

29
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:40.227
Your turn. Say: Workers.

30
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:44.079
Workers.

31
00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:46.027
Supply chain.

32
00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:51.048
Supply chain means the network that moves a product from maker to buyer.

33
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.613
The supply chain stretches across three countries.

34
00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:58.020
Your turn. Say: Supply chain.

35
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:02.239
Supply chain.

36
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:03.926
Wages.

37
00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:07.555
Wages means money paid to workers for their work.

38
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.324
Fair wages reduce worker turnover.

39
00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:13.221
Your turn. Say: Wages.

40
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:17.219
Wages.

41
00:02:17.983 --> 00:02:19.008
Standards.

42
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:23.851
Standards means official levels of quality, safety or behaviour.

43
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.926
International standards protect workers.

44
00:02:27.517 --> 00:02:30.053
Your turn. Say: Standards.

45
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:33.843
Standards.

46
00:02:34.633 --> 00:02:38.087
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:46.165
Visal, a report says 'The factory had its machines inspected and got its safety standards checked.' Who did the inspecting?

48
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:57.022
Not the factory. That is the causative: have / get + object + past participle. It means someone else did the job for us.

49
00:02:58.117 --> 00:03:00.337
What is the difference between have and get?

50
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:10.163
Have is neutral and slightly formal; get is more informal. 'Brands have their suppliers audited' means an inspector audits them.

51
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.946
Why is it useful for manufacturing news?

52
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:25.478
Because reports focus on actions done to the factory: having standards verified, getting wages reviewed, having products tested before export.

53
00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:28.331
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.183 --> 00:03:34.143
Grammar focus: Causative have and get plus object plus past participle.

55
00:03:34.443 --> 00:03:35.607
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.317 --> 00:03:38.641
The factory had its machines inspected.

57
00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.049
The brand has its suppliers audited every year.

58
00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.402
Workers got their wages reviewed by the union.

59
00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:55.242
Causative: have/get + object + past participle means someone else does the action. Do not use the base verb: had it repaired, not had it repair.

60
00:03:55.983 --> 00:03:59.251
Be careful! Don't say: She had her car repair.

61
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.385
Say: She had her car repaired.

62
00:04:03.685 --> 00:04:06.567
Use the past participle after have + object.

63
00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:09.926
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.733 --> 00:04:12.640
Inside a Garment Factory.

65
00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:16.727
Manufacturing is one of the largest employers in the garment industry.

66
00:04:17.733 --> 00:04:23.367
A factory that sells exports abroad often has its safety standards checked by outside auditors.

67
00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:29.687
Global brands have their suppliers audited every year, and workers get their wages reviewed with the union.

68
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:34.940
Each garment must meet international standards before it leaves the supply chain.

69
00:04:35.933 --> 00:04:39.233
Fair conditions keep both workers and buyers satisfied.

70
00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:44.196
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.630
The factory blank its machines inspected.

72
00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.922
A, had. B, made. C, was.

73
00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.176
The answer is A: had.

74
00:04:57.317 --> 00:04:59.235
A piece of clothing is a blank.

75
00:04:59.535 --> 00:05:03.142
A, garment. B, factory. C, standard.

76
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.316
The answer is A: garment.

77
00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:13.052
Official levels of quality, safety or behaviour are blank.

78
00:05:13.352 --> 00:05:17.272
A, standards. B, exports. C, wages.

79
00:05:20.833 --> 00:05:23.262
The answer is A: standards.

80
00:05:24.417 --> 00:05:26.080
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:33.144
A, She had her car repair.. B, She had her car repaired.. C, She had repaired her car by a mechanic..

82
00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.524
The answer is B: She had her car repaired..

83
00:05:40.683 --> 00:05:44.645
According to the reading, fair conditions keep workers and buyers…

84
00:05:44.945 --> 00:05:48.831
A, satisfied. B, silent. C, away.

85
00:05:52.367 --> 00:05:54.331
The answer is A: satisfied.

86
00:05:55.533 --> 00:05:58.766
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:59.617 --> 00:06:02.545
Why are labour standards important in manufacturing?

88
00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:12.885
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:13.185 --> 00:06:38.680
Labour standards are important because factory workers need safe conditions, fair wages and reasonable hours. Without them, accidents and exploitation become more likely. They also protect a country's reputation, since global brands prefer suppliers that have their factories inspected. In this way, good standards help both workers and exports.

90
00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:40.526
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:41.417 --> 00:06:47.598
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and causative have and get plus object plus past participle.

92
00:06:47.898 --> 00:06:52.350
Today's value is fairness. Look for solutions that are fair to everyone.

93
00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:55.442
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:06:55.742 --> 00:06:59.126
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

